course: dictionary
lesson: 8530
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A saver is a person or thing that saves. The word comes from the verb save, and the -er ending means "one who does": a saver saves, a teacher teaches. Savers keep money aside instead of spending it all, so a bank can pay them a little extra money called interest.
explain: The same word can mean different things in different places. «Your tap and your toilet are among the greatest life-savers ever invented.» Here a life-saver is not a person in the sea. It is something that helps a lot, and nobody could manage without it.
explain: Context tells you which meaning to pick. In a money lesson, saver means a person with a bank account. On a computer, a screen saver is a moving picture that shows when the screen is not being used. Read the words around the word first, then choose the meaning that fits.
fact: A saver is a person who keeps money instead of spending it all.
fact: A time-saver or money-saver is something that saves you time or money.
fact: A screen saver is a moving picture that a computer shows when it is not being used.
fact: In sport, a saver is a goalkeeper or player who stops goals or points.
fact: «Life-saver» means something that helps you a lot, like a tap or a toilet in everyday life.
exercise: «Your tap and your toilet are among the greatest life-savers ever invented.» What does life-saver mean here? | A bank account | *Something that helps a lot, like a tap or a toilet | A goalkeeper who stops goals | A person who swims in the sea
exercise: Maya puts her pocket money in a piggy bank each week and does not spend it all. What is Maya? | *A saver | A screen saver | A goalkeeper | A time-saver
exercise: Dad says his laptop shows a moving picture when he stops using it. What is this called? | A penalty saver | A money saver | A time-saver | *A screen saver
test: In football, what do we call a player who stops goals from going in? | A dribbler | *A saver | A winger | A striker
test: «A time-saver» means | A clock that is always wrong | A slow walker | Something that wastes time | *Something that saves you time
exam: In «If a bank pays a saver £2 on £100», how much does the saver earn? | £7 | £5 | *£2 | £100
